.Dd August 2026
.Dt MORIARTY\-TOOL\-RULES 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm moriarty\-tool\-rules
.Nd tool call permissioning rules for moriarty
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Tool rules permission any Claude Code tool call \(em not just Bash.
They are configured as
.Ic [[tool_rules]]
entries in
.Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml
and are checked
.Em before
bash rules, providing a unified way to control tool access.
.Pp
Both tool rules and bash rules coexist in the same configuration file.
See
.Xr moriarty\-bash\-rules 5
for bash\-specific rules.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
.Ss File Location
.Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml
.Pp
The path respects
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
if set.
.Ss Rule Structure
Each tool rule is a TOML array\-of\-tables entry:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[tool_rules]]
name = "descriptive\-name"
tool = "ToolName"
modes = ["default", "plan"] # optional
allow_local = true          # optional
field = "field_name"       # optional legacy pair
pattern = "regex\-pattern"
conditions = [              # optional; all must match
  { type = "Present", field = "request" },
  { type = "Absent", field = "dangerous" },
  { type = "Equals", field = "enabled", value = true },
  { type = "Matches", field = "path", pattern = "^src/" },
]
action = { type = "ActionType", value = "..." }
.Ed
.Ss Fields
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic name
A descriptive name for the rule, used in log output.
.It Ic tool
Exact tool name to match, or
.Ql *
to match any tool.
Known tools include:
.Cm Read ,
.Cm Write ,
.Cm Edit ,
.Cm Bash ,
.Cm Glob ,
and
.Cm Grep .
.It Ic modes
Optional permission\-mode allow\-list.
Accepted values are exactly
.Cm default ,
.Cm plan ,
.Cm acceptEdits ,
.Cm auto ,
.Cm dontAsk ,
and
.Cm bypassPermissions .
The UI's Manual mode arrives as
.Cm default ;
.Cm manual
is invalid.
Omitting the field permanently defines an unrestricted rule enabled in every mode;
this is a fully supported configuration, not a deprecated compatibility form.
An empty array disables the rule.
Current hook events require a permission mode.
Ineligible rules are skipped in place, so a matching restricted rule falls through to the next eligible tool rule and,
for Bash, eventually to the bash engine with the same mode.
.It Ic allow_local
Optional.
When set to
.Cm true ,
the rule only matches if the relevant path field resolves to a canonical path within the hook's canonicalized
.Ic cwd .
For a condition\-free legacy rule, a
.Ic field
of
.Ql path
or
.Ql file_path
must be local; with no legacy field either local path field is sufficient, while a non\-path legacy field cannot satisfy locality.
For a rule with
.Ic conditions ,
every distinct
.Ql path
or
.Ql file_path
referenced by
.Cm Present ,
.Cm Equals ,
or
.Cm Matches ,
or by the legacy field when it names one of those path keys, must be local.
.Cm Absent
does not select a path.
If no path is selected, the legacy fallback applies.
Relative paths are resolved against
.Ic cwd .
Existing paths are fully canonicalized; non\-existent paths are checked by canonicalizing the deepest existing ancestor and safely rebuilding the missing suffix so
.Ql ..
cannot escape above that ancestor.
Symlinks that resolve outside
.Ic cwd
are rejected, and broken symlinks are treated as non\-local.
Hard links are treated as ordinary local filesystem entries and are not distinguished from regular files.
In the live hook, if locality inspection does not finish within two seconds, every
.Ic allow_local
rule is skipped and evaluation falls through to a later rule or no match;
synchronous analysis runs without this latency deadline.
.It Ic field
Optional.
The name of a field in the tool input to match against.
Must be paired with
.Ic pattern ;
if only one is present, the rule is skipped and a configuration error is logged.
.It Ic pattern
Optional.
A regular expression
.Pq Rust regex syntax
to match against the value of
.Ic field .
Must be paired with
.Ic field .
Pattern fragments
.Pq see Xr moriarty\-bash\-rules 5
may be used with
.Ql {{fragment_name}}
syntax.
If
.Ic allow_local
is also enabled, both checks must pass.
.It Ic conditions
Optional list of typed predicates over literal top\-level tool input keys.
Every condition is ANDed with every other condition and with the tool name,
.Ic allow_local ,
and the legacy
.Ic field Ns / Ns Ic pattern
pair.
Separate ordered rules provide alternatives and fallback behavior.
See
.Sx CONDITIONS .
.It Ic action
The action to take when the rule matches.
One of
.Cm Allow ,
.Cm Deny ,
or
.Cm Ask .
See
.Sx ACTIONS .
.El
.Ss CONDITIONS
A rule with conditions matches only when the tool input is a JSON object.
Dots in a condition's
.Ic field
name are literal and do not denote a JSON path.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm Present
The named top\-level key exists, regardless of its value; a key whose value is JSON null is present.
.It Cm Absent
The named top\-level key does not exist.
For object inputs this is the exact inverse of
.Cm Present .
.It Cm Equals
The key exists and its raw JSON value equals the configured JSON\-compatible TOML
.Ic value .
Equality is recursive and type\-sensitive: boolean
.Cm true
differs from string
.Ql true ,
and arrays and inline tables may be compared exactly.
.It Cm Matches
The named scalar value matches
.Ic pattern
using the same extraction, cwd\-prefix stripping, and pattern\-fragment expansion as the legacy field/pattern pair.
Strings match directly; numbers and booleans are stringified; arrays, objects, and null do not match.
.El
.Pp
Missing or extra condition properties are configuration errors.
If any
.Cm Matches
pattern cannot expand or compile, the entire rule is dropped and reported; Moriarty never retains only the valid conditions because that could broaden an Allow rule.
Use
.Cm moriarty rules lint
to detect dropped rules.
.Ss Field Pattern Matching
When
.Ic field
and
.Ic pattern
are both specified,
.Nm moriarty
extracts the field value from the tool input:
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Em Strings :
matched directly
.Pq e.g., file_path, content .
.It
.Em Numbers :
converted to string
.Pq e.g., 42 becomes \(lq42\(rq .
.It
.Em Booleans :
converted to
.Ql true
or
.Ql false .
.It
.Em Arrays, Objects, Null :
cannot be matched; the rule does not apply.
.El
.Ss CWD Prefix Stripping
Claude Code sends absolute paths in tool inputs
.Pq e.g., /home/user/project/src/main.rs .
Before regex matching,
.Nm moriarty
strips the hook input's
.Ic cwd
prefix from field values, so rules can use relative paths.
For example, with
.Ic cwd = \(lq/home/user/project\(rq ,
the field value
.Pa /home/user/project/src/main.rs
becomes
.Pa src/main.rs
for matching purposes.
If the value does not start with
.Ic cwd ,
it is matched as\-is.
.Pp
When
.Ic allow_local
is enabled, a path regex uses the canonicalized local path before cwd stripping, while a non\-path regex still reads the raw input.
.Cm Equals
always compares the raw JSON value, including for path fields; locality is a separate gate.
.Sh ACTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm Allow
Permit the tool call without user confirmation.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.It Cm Deny
Block the tool call and display an error message.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
action = { type = "Deny", value = "reason message" }
.Ed
.It Cm Ask
Defer to the user for case\-by\-case authorization.
Claude Code will prompt the user to approve or deny.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
action = { type = "Ask" }
.Ed
.El
.Pp
Note:
.Cm Modify
and
.Cm ArgumentFilter
actions are only available in bash rules.
.Sh EVALUATION ORDER
.Bd -literal -offset indent
PreToolUse event (any tool)
  |
  +\-> tool_rules engine (first\-match\-wins among eligible rules)
  |     permission mode is eligible?
  |       \-> tool matches?
  |         \-> allow_local check (if enabled)
  |         \-> every condition matches (if configured)
  |         \-> legacy field/pattern regex matches (if configured)
  |     Match found? \-> return Allow/Deny/Ask
  |     No match?    \-> continue
  |
  +\-> tool_name == "Bash"?
  |     Yes \-> bash_rules engine
  |     No  \-> defer to Claude Code (no decision)
.Ed
.Pp
Rules are evaluated in order.
Mode eligibility is checked before tool, locality, condition, and regex gates, so an ineligible
.Ic allow_local
rule does not trigger filesystem preflight.
The first eligible matching rule determines the action
.Pq first\-match\-wins semantics .
.Sh EXAMPLES
Allow read\-only tools, deny writing to
.Pa .env
files, ask for everything else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[tool_rules]]
name = "allow\-read"
tool = "Read"
action = { type = "Allow" }

[[tool_rules]]
name = "allow\-glob"
tool = "Glob"
action = { type = "Allow" }

[[tool_rules]]
name = "deny\-write\-env"
tool = "Write"
field = "file_path"
pattern = "\e\e.env$"
action = { type = "Deny", value = "Cannot write to .env files" }

[[tool_rules]]
name = "ask\-unknown"
tool = "*"
action = { type = "Ask" }
.Ed
.Pp
Using pattern fragments:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[pattern_fragments]
project = "/home/user/project"

[[tool_rules]]
name = "allow\-project\-read"
tool = "Read"
field = "file_path"
pattern = "^{{project}}/"
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.Pp
Restrict writes to local Rust source files under the current working directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[tool_rules]]
name = "allow\-local\-src\-writes"
tool = "Write"
allow_local = true
field = "file_path"
pattern = "^src/.*\e\.rs$"
action = { type = "Allow" }
.Ed
.Pp
Require ordinary subagent execution starts to use top\-level
.Ic async = true
and omit top\-level
.Ic turnBudget ,
while leaving action, status, and management calls outside the policy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[[tool_rules]]
name = "allow\-valid\-subagent\-start"
tool = "subagent"
conditions = [
  { type = "Absent", field = "action" },
  { type = "Equals", field = "async", value = true },
  { type = "Absent", field = "turnBudget" },
]
action = { type = "Allow" }

[[tool_rules]]
name = "deny\-invalid\-subagent\-start"
tool = "subagent"
conditions = [{ type = "Absent", field = "action" }]
action = { type = "Deny", value = "Normal subagent starts require async=true and must omit turnBudget" }
.Ed
.Pp
The Allow rule must come first because rules are first\-match\-wins.
An input key
.Ic action
whose value is null is still present and bypasses both rules, while any present
.Ic turnBudget ,
including null, is denied.
Unrelated execution fields do not affect the policy.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/moriarty/tool_rules.toml
Tool and bash rules configuration file.
.It Pa ~/.local/state/moriarty/hooks/
Hook execution logs (JSON lines) showing rule evaluation details.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr moriarty 1 ,
.Xr moriarty\-bash\-rules 5
